-- allium: 3
-- store-split.allium
--
-- Scope: The split of responsibility between the dispatch TUI process and
--        the SpacetimeDB module (spacetime/module/), and the request rows
--        through which the module drives work that needs a host's machine.
--        The module owns each request row outright, so the row's entity and
--        its lifecycle live here rather than in a domain spec: the dispatch
--        request (DispatchRequest), the session request for wrap-up,
--        exit-session and the done-path close (SessionRequest), and the feed
--        request for a refresh or a teardown (FeedRequest). dispatch.allium,
--        mcp-task-tools.allium, pr-workflow.allium, epics.allium,
--        tasks.allium, feeds.allium, sync.allium and spacetime-seed.allium
--        point back here. The module's other data rules live in the spec of
--        the data they guard: task writes in tasks.allium, epic and
--        work-package updates in epics.allium and work-packages.allium, PR
--        state in pr-workflow.allium, poll ownership in feeds.allium.
-- Includes: The split rule, the "host work stays thin" rule, the request-row
--           model for multi-step work, the version gate, the decisions every
--           request row follows (D1–D12, W1–W13, P1–P5, X1–X6 below), the
--           dispatch request row and its lifecycle, the session request row
--           (wrap-up, exit-session and close) and its lifecycle,
--           update_tasks' done-path close and its close row, the feed
--           request row (a refresh asked of an epic's poll owner, a teardown
--           asked of the host holding a feed task the stale delete removed)
--           and its lifecycle, the owner check on host-scoped setting writes,
--           and the details still undecided.
-- Excludes: What a host does to provision a claimed dispatch request — the
--           worktree, the tmux window, the launch (dispatch.allium:
--           ProvisionDispatch); what a host does to serve a claimed session
--           request — the rebase (pr-workflow.allium: WrapUpRebase) and the
--           window teardown (pr-workflow.allium: ExitSessionTeardown); what a
--           session close writes to the task (pr-workflow.allium:
--           ExitSession, which runs in the module inside RequestExitSession's
--           transaction); the rest of wrap-up and PR handling
--           (pr-workflow.allium);
--           what a host does to serve a claimed feed request — the feed
--           cycle and the teardown (feeds.allium: ServeFeedRefreshRequest,
--           ServeFeedTeardownRequest);
--           feed execution and routing (feeds.allium, feed-scripts.allium);
--           what a host does after a PR merge (pr-workflow.allium: PrMerged);
--           the store's
--           subscription and connection mechanics, including the asks that
--           deliver dispatch and session requests to their host (sync.allium:
--           SubscribeToThisHostsDispatchRequests,
--           SubscribeToThisHostsSessionRequests,
--           SubscribeToThisHostsFeedRequests); startup checks
--           (startup.allium); the MCP tools (mcp-task-tools.allium).
-- Dependencies: sync.allium holds the version gate's rule
--               (AStoreWithOtherRulesFailsTheAttempt) and the rules version
--               itself (config.rules_version). settings.allium raises the
--               setting writes this spec owner-checks.
--
-- Decisions. Other specs cite these ids.
--
--   - S1, the split rule. A piece of behaviour stays in the TUI process only
--     if it needs the operator's machine: git, tmux, launching claude, gh,
--     local repo files, or the local embedding model. Every rule about the
--     shared data's shape, consistency or state transitions belongs in the
--     SpacetimeDB module's reducers, so every client gets the same rule
--     rather than each client re-implementing it. Checks about the request
--     rather than the data (an empty patch, the feed interval floor, the
--     typed-url pair) stay at the service and MCP boundary; the module still
--     refuses a missing epic itself (tasks.allium:
--     UpdateTaskIsOneTransaction, epics.allium: UpdateEpicIsOneTransaction).
--   - S2, host work stays in the TUI process, and stays thin. The MCP and
--     hook HTTP endpoint, feed script execution and PR polling run in the
--     TUI process. There is no separate daemon: agents need a board running.
--     Each of these only performs the local call and forwards the raw result
--     through one reducer call. None of them decides a state change; the
--     module does.
--   - S3, the module drives multi-step work. Dispatch, wrap-up and feed
--     work start as request rows; exit-session and the done-path close apply
--     the close in the module and write a request row for the host's
--     teardown that follows it.
--     The module sets each state of the request (for example requested ->
--     provisioning on host X -> dispatched | failed). The host that owns the
--     work reacts through its subscription, does the local work, and reports
--     the outcome by reducer call. A scheduled reducer fails dispatch and
--     session work that its host never reports, so the timeout holds whether
--     or not the board that asked is still running.
--   - The version gate. A board on an older build would apply old rules
--     against a newer module, or fail to apply rules the module expects a
--     host to follow. The module publishes a RULES VERSION, one integer in a
--     one-row table of its own (a table, so a module without the gate answers
--     "no such table" rather than a row the board cannot decode), stamped by
--     the module from its own constant whenever a client connects. The board
--     compares it for EQUALITY with the version compiled into the board, at
--     every connection attempt, and refuses to run on a difference
--     (sync.allium: AStoreWithOtherRulesFailsTheAttempt). The number is held
--     twice, in the module crate and in the board, with a test that fails
--     when they differ; spacetime/module/src/rules.rs::RULES_VERSION says
--     what warrants a bump. The wasm hash is not the gate because it changes
--     on every rebuild whether or not a rule moved; a build version is not,
--     because it needs a stored minimum that someone also has to bump.
--     Limit: a board built without the gate cannot refuse; it still meets
--     the module's own checks.
--
--   Dispatch requests (DispatchRequest below):
--   - D1, the row. One dispatch_request row per request, not columns on the
--     task. It carries a kind column whose only value is dispatch. A task
--     holds at most one OPEN request (requested or provisioning) at a time;
--     finished rows (dispatched or failed) stay as history until
--     RequestRowsArePruned removes them. An optional column is stored as ""
--     for absent, not as an Option, and every timestamp comes from the
--     module clock.
--   - D2, the flow. Any client calls the request reducer. The module claims
--     the task atomically (backlog -> running, so DispatchClaimExclusive
--     holds: a second request for a claimed task is refused) and writes the
--     row. The row's host is ALWAYS THE REQUESTING HOST, never the task's:
--     you request work on a task, you provision it on your own machine, so
--     the module refuses a task whose worktree is on another machine. The
--     host's board claims the row (requested -> provisioning), provisions the
--     worktree, tmux window and agent, and reports either dispatched (the
--     module writes the task's worktree, window and host) or failed (the
--     module releases the claim conditionally).
--   - D3, the mode. mode is a column (dispatch | research | quick). The
--     requester may pass it; when it does not, the module derives it (a plan
--     -> dispatch, else the research tag -> research, else dispatch). quick
--     is never derived: only quick dispatch names it, for its own prompt
--     variant. The host reads the mode from the row and never re-derives it.
--     MCP, the epic chain and retry-fresh name no mode (dispatch.allium:
--     RetryFresh), so a retried research task stays research.
--   - D4, the timeout. ONE deadline per request,
--     config.dispatch_request_timeout from requested_at, whatever state the
--     row is in, exposed on the row. THE HOST ENFORCES IT: provisioning stops
--     at the deadline, any subprocess still running is killed, what the
--     attempt made is rolled back as for a provisioning failure, and the host
--     reports failed with reason "deadline passed" (dispatch.allium:
--     ProvisioningStopsAtTheDeadline). The module's timer is only a BACKSTOP
--     for a host that is gone or hung: one scheduled row per request,
--     cancelled when the request finishes, due
--     config.dispatch_request_backstop_grace after the deadline, so a live
--     host's stop lands first. When it fires on a row still open the module
--     fails the row "timed out" and releases the claim. A dispatched report
--     arriving after the row has failed is refused, and the host tears down
--     what it made. UnprovisionedIndicator's claim-stamp fallback uses the
--     same config value.
--   - D5, catch-up. A requested row is picked up from its host's initial
--     subscription rows when that host's board starts. A row already
--     provisioning when its host restarts is NOT resumed: the host's startup
--     reports it failed ("host restarted"), which releases the claim. There
--     is one board per host: startup restarts the board inside the single
--     dispatch tmux session (startup.allium), so a starting board's
--     provisioning rows are never a live sibling's.
--   - D6, the callers. Every launch path that provisions writes a request
--     row: the TUI dispatch keys, retry-fresh, research, quick dispatch
--     (tasks.allium: CreateQuickTask creates the task, then requests), MCP
--     dispatch_task, the epic auto-dispatch chain (epics.allium:
--     AutoDispatchNextSubtask, which advances on a refused request and
--     reports AutoDispatchFailed from the row's outcome) and a board firing a
--     due scheduled task (schedules.allium: FireDueTask). dispatch_task writes
--     the request and waits up to 30 seconds for dispatched or failed; when
--     the wait ends first it returns the request id and its state, and
--     get_task shows the request state. The TUI's DispatchingFeedback follows
--     the row.
--   - D7, the gate. Every rule the module enforces for dispatch requests is
--     covered by the version gate above (sync.allium: config.rules_version).
--   - D8, deletion. Deleting a task with an open request fails that request
--     in the same reducer (reason "task deleted") and cancels its timer, in
--     every module reducer that deletes a task row: DeleteTask, BatchDelete,
--     an epic delete's subtree and the stale-subtree delete in ingest_feed.
--     The same transaction then deletes the row
--     (DeletingATaskDeletesItsRequestRows), so no reader sees the reason. A
--     host still provisioning learns of it when its dispatched report is
--     refused, and tears down whatever it made (dispatch.allium:
--     ProvisionDispatch).
--   - D9, origin. The row records which launch path wrote it (user | mcp |
--     epic_chain | quick | schedule; user covers the TUI keys, retry-fresh
--     and research; schedule is a board firing a due task). An epic_chain row
--     that fails raises AutoDispatchFailed from the row itself, on the
--     requesting host's board only (epics.allium:
--     ReportChainedDispatchFailure), so a board restart during the chain's
--     wait does not lose it.
--   - D10, snapshots. dispatch_request and its timer table are in-flight
--     state and are not in any snapshot (spacetime-seed.allium:
--     SharedTable); a restore loses request history, by decision.
--   - D11, who may act for a host. A request makes its host's board run git,
--     tmux and claude, so a client may name a host — as the requester, the
--     claimer or a reporter — only when that host's registry row exists with
--     an owner and the owner is the caller's own identity (caller(), the
--     store's view of who sent the call). A host with no owner yet is
--     refused. The module records the caller on the row as requested_by, and
--     a host's board serves only rows requested_by its own owner, as a second
--     line behind the module's check (dispatch.allium: ProvisionDispatch).
--     The scheduled backstop is the module's own call and keeps its own
--     check. The owner is a fact these checks can rest on because the host
--     registry's own write enforces core.allium's
--     LocalHostOwnerIsWrittenOnce: a caller may name only itself as a host's
--     owner, and an owned host's owner is never rewritten. A NEW host row
--     must name its owner: host ids are public, so an ownerless row would go
--     to whichever caller registered that id next, and a teammate could lock
--     a new machine out of its own host. A legacy ownerless row may be
--     adopted by the first caller naming itself. The board registers only
--     once its identity has settled, and always as that identity
--     (sync.allium: RegisterHostOnConnect). LIMIT, by decision: one person's
--     machines share an owner, so one of their machines can name another of
--     theirs; a teammate cannot. Every other reducer that takes a host
--     argument makes the same check, for example poll ownership (feeds.allium:
--     ClaimPollOwner, OverridePollOwner), PR reports (pr-workflow.allium:
--     ReportPrStatus, ReportPrUnreadable), the feed reducers (ingest_feed and
--     the FeedRequest rules below), host-scoped settings (SaveSettingForHost
--     below) and the repo-path and verify-command writes. A task's own host
--     column gets a weaker, related check: a patch or create may not aim it
--     at another person's host, but a host nobody owns is not judged
--     (tasks.allium: PatchTaskNeverAimsAnotherHostsTeardown,
--     CreateTaskNeverAimsAnotherHostsTeardown).
--   - D12, failures while no board ran. A request whose backstop fired while
--     its host's board was closed is already failed in the starting board's
--     baseline, and the starting board does NOT raise it: neither
--     FailedRequestReported nor epics.allium's ReportChainedDispatchFailure
--     fires for it, epic_chain rows included, and a chain stalled that way
--     gets no ChainStallMarked. The failed card shows it: the task is back in
--     backlog and the row records the reason.
--
--   Session requests: wrap-up, exit-session and close (SessionRequest below):
--   - W1, the row. One session_request row (accessor session_requests) per
--     wrap_up, exit_session or close request, plus a schedule table for its
--     backstop (session_request_timeouts). A table of its own, not a kind of
--     dispatch_request: its lifecycle is requested -> running -> done |
--     failed, it carries an action, a pr url and a token rather than a mode,
--     an origin or a worktree, and its failure releases no claim. Folding it
--     in would leave half of every row's columns meaningless for its kind and
--     make every dispatch reader filter by kind. wrap_up_request (the wrap_up
--     row an exit_session row consumed), tmux_window (the window to tear
--     down) and chained_task (the subtask the epic chain requested) carry
--     what the flow needs. An optional column is stored as "" for absent, as
--     in D1, and every timestamp comes from the module clock. A task holds at
--     most one OPEN session request (requested or running) of any kind;
--     finished rows stay as history until RequestRowsArePruned removes them.
--   - W2, the wrap_up flow. The MCP handler first runs the host-local checks,
--     BEFORE writing anything: the task is wrappable, its worktree names a
--     branch (rebase only), the worktree is committed
--     (WrapUpRefusesUnfinishedWork). A refusal there writes no row and issues
--     no token. Then it calls request_wrap_up(task, host, action), host being
--     this board's own. The module checks D11, that the task is running or
--     review with a worktree on that host, that it is not review-tagged
--     (mcp-task-tools.allium's ReviewTasksAreNotWrappedUp, the module's by
--     decision; the tool may keep the same check as an early refusal with
--     the same wording), and that the task has no open session request
--     (RequestWrapUp). For action rebase the row is requested; the owning
--     host claims it (requested -> running), which clears a Conflict
--     sub_status in the same transaction (HostClaimsSessionRequest); runs the
--     rebase (pr-workflow.allium: WrapUpRebase); and reports done, or failed
--     with a reason and whether the failure was a rebase conflict. THE MODULE
--     sets sub_status = Conflict on a conflict report
--     (HostReportsSessionRequestFailed, then pr-workflow.allium:
--     FinishTaskConflict). For done and pr there is no host work: the module
--     writes the row already done, in the same call, and schedules no timer.
--   - W3, the token. A done wrap_up row is the proof exit_session needs: the
--     token shown to the agent is that row's id. Only the task's most recent
--     done wrap_up row is valid, and each is consumed once. It survives a
--     board restart. A LATER DISPATCH VOIDS IT (decided with the user): a
--     token is valid only if its wrap_up row finished after the finished_at
--     of the task's latest dispatched request, so a token from an earlier
--     session of a re-dispatched task cannot close the new session. A voided
--     token is refused with "invalid exit token".
--   - W4, the exit_session flow. exit_session calls
--     request_exit_session(task, token, action, pr_url). The module requires
--     a valid token for the task (W3) whose action matches, and a pr_url for
--     action pr (RequestExitSession). In the same call it applies the
--     terminal state (rebase or done -> Done, pr -> Review with the pr url;
--     completed_at from the store clock; pr-workflow.allium: ExitSession),
--     rolls up the epic chain, consumes the token, and writes an exit_session
--     row naming the task's host and its tmux window for the teardown. The
--     close is one transaction, so it took effect whole or not at all, and a
--     refused or failed call leaves the token valid for a retry. exit_session
--     does NOT wait for the teardown (decided with the user): the teardown
--     kills the calling agent's own window, so a reply that waited for it
--     would never be read (mcp-task-tools.allium: ExitSessionViaMcp).
--   - W5, the teardown. The host's board claims the exit_session or close
--     row, kills the window and reports done, or failed with a reason. A
--     failed teardown does NOT revert the close; it is recorded on the row
--     and logged (pr-workflow.allium: ExitSessionTeardown). The host serving
--     the teardown row also runs ClosePrOnDone's gh pr close for a close that
--     took a task with a pr-typed url into Done, reading the url from the
--     task. A row that fails before its host serves it (the backstop, the
--     restart sweep, the task's deletion, a close that fails an earlier row)
--     leaves the PR open: KillWindowOfFailedExitSession kills the window
--     only. Accepted as built.
--   - W6, the epic chain. The module owns the epic chain's next
--     auto-dispatch: AutoDispatchNextSubtask's selection runs in
--     request_exit_session's transaction and writes a dispatch request with
--     origin epic_chain naming the same host (epics.allium:
--     AutoDispatchNextSubtask), only for a close that had a wrap_up behind
--     it. The exit_session row records the chained subtask for the response.
--     ReportChainedDispatchFailure stays on the requesting board.
--   - W7, the timeouts. As D4: ONE deadline per row from requested_at,
--     exposed on the row; THE HOST ENFORCES IT, and the module's timer is
--     only a backstop. A wrap_up rebase row has
--     config.wrap_up_rebase_timeout, an exit_session or close teardown
--     config.exit_session_teardown_timeout, and the backstop fires
--     config.session_request_backstop_grace after the deadline. A row the
--     backstop finds open fails "timed out". A timed-out wrap_up row leaves
--     the task as it was: the Conflict flag is cleared only by the claim and
--     not restored. Short budgets on purpose: the wrap_up tool waits on the
--     row, and an agent stuck in a call is worse than one told to try again.
--   - W8, catch-up and restart. As D5: a requested row is picked up from its
--     host's initial subscription rows when the board starts, and a row
--     already running when its host restarts is not resumed: the starting
--     board fails it ("host restarted")
--     (HostRestartFailsInterruptedSessionRequests). For an exit_session or
--     close row the starting board then still kills the window the row
--     names, best effort (pr-workflow.allium: ExitSessionTeardown).
--   - W9, deletion. As D8: deleting a task fails its open session request
--     ("task deleted") in the same reducer and cancels its timer, in every
--     module reducer that deletes a task row (DeleteTask, BatchDelete, an
--     epic delete's subtree and the stale-subtree delete in ingest_feed).
--   - W10, snapshots. As D10: session_request and its timer table are
--     in-flight state and are not in any snapshot (spacetime-seed.allium:
--     SharedTable). A restore loses every unconsumed exit token with the
--     rows; the agent calls wrap_up again.
--   - W11, who may act for a host. D11 applies to every reducer that names a
--     host: request_wrap_up (its host argument), request_exit_session (the
--     task's host, which the row names), close_task's row (X1), the claim,
--     both reports and the restart sweep. The row records requested_by, and
--     a board serves only rows requested_by its own owner. The scheduled
--     backstop keeps its own check.
--   - W12, the gate. Every rule the module enforces for session requests is
--     covered by the version gate above (sync.allium: config.rules_version).
--   - W13, what stays on the board. The host-local pre-checks (W2), the
--     rebase and the teardown, ClosePrOnDone's gh call (W5), the MCP waits
--     and the response text.
--
--   PR state (pr-workflow.allium: ReportPrStatus, ReportPrUnreadable):
--   - P1, no watchers. There are no task watchers; the dead task_watchers
--     table stays in the module only so a publish is not refused.
--   - P2, one reducer. The host runs gh and posts what it read in raw form
--     (report_pr_status: the state and reviewDecision strings as gh printed
--     them) or that it could not read the PR (report_pr_unreadable). The
--     module parses, validates and derives every task write: merged -> done,
--     closed -> pr_closed, open -> the review sub_status, unreadable ->
--     pr_unreachable.
--   - P3, counters stay on the host. The consecutive-failure counters and the
--     backoff pace this machine's gh calls and are session-scoped on purpose
--     (pr-workflow.allium: PrPollState). The give-up THRESHOLD is a host
--     constant; the module decides only what a give-up report does to the
--     task.
--   - P4, effects after a merge. The module sets the task Done (completed_at,
--     epic rollup) and clears its window. The host, which read the window
--     before posting, then kills it, sends the desktop notice and respawns a
--     phoenix task's split pane. No teardown row: the work is best-effort and
--     the module needs no answer.
--   - P5, the gate. The PR rules are covered by the version gate above.
--
--   Rules the module owns outright (no board-side copy):
--   - X1, the update_tasks done path (mcp-task-tools.allium:
--     MarkTaskDoneViaMcp). The tool keeps its request-shape checks at the MCP
--     boundary (status must be the only field; a dispatched agent may not
--     close its own task) and then calls close_task(task_id, host), host
--     being this board's own (RequestCloseTask). In ONE transaction the
--     module applies the terminal Done write — ExitSession's terminal fields
--     with action done (pr-workflow.allium), except that a done -> done
--     re-close keeps the task's completed_at — clears stop_pending when the
--     task leaves running, clears the window, rolls up the epic chain, and
--     respawns a phoenix task that enters Done (tasks.allium:
--     PhoenixRespawn). It issues, checks and consumes NO exit token and runs
--     NO epic chain. It writes a session request of kind close ONLY when
--     there is host work (a live window or a pr-typed url) and the teardown
--     host is owned by the caller (D11); otherwise the task closes with no
--     row, because a window on a teammate's machine is not ours to kill. The
--     host serves a close row exactly as an exit_session teardown (W5).
--     KillWindowOfFailedExitSession, the teardown timeout and its backstop,
--     the restart sweep and the task-deletion failure apply to close rows as
--     to exit_session rows. An OPEN session or dispatch request on the task
--     is failed "task closed" in the same transaction rather than refusing
--     the close (CloseFailsOpenRequests): a stuck wrap_up must not block
--     closing, and W1's one-open-row rule holds because the old row is
--     failed before the close row is written. EveryExitSessionHadAWrapUp
--     applies to exit_session rows only. The tool waits for nothing.
--   - X2, patch_task owns the transition rules. When a patch moves a task
--     out of running the module clears stop_pending in the same write
--     (core.allium: PendingStopOnlyWhileRunning) unless the patch names
--     stop_pending itself, in which case the patch's value stands; when a
--     patch moves a task into Done from another status it runs the phoenix
--     respawn in the same transaction (tasks.allium: PhoenixRespawn). The
--     module stamps completed_at on entering Done unless a patch_task patch
--     names one (tasks.allium: UpdateTaskIsOneTransaction). The board never stamps,
--     clears or respawns itself; a board that needs the stamp re-reads the
--     task.
--   - X3, epic rollups. The module is the only place that recalculates an
--     epic's status (epics.allium: EpicStatusRecalculation): every mutation
--     rolls the epic up in its own transaction, and the board never
--     recalculates.
--   - X4, managed epics. Provisioning (epics.allium: ProvisionManagedEpics)
--     is one reducer, ensure_managed_epics(reviews_command,
--     reviews_interval_secs, cve_command, cve_interval_secs), so two boards
--     starting together cannot create duplicates. The board reads and writes
--     the settings and applies the feed-interval floor, which is request
--     shape, and calls the reducer.
--   - X5, what stays at the boundary. The learning create's summary check,
--     its code-citation check and the feed-interval floor are request shape
--     and stay at the service and MCP boundary (S1). The learning scope shape
--     is checked at both. The learning verdict's "not retrieved" check, the
--     work-package checks and their multi-part update (work-packages.allium:
--     UpdateWorkPackageIsOneTransaction) and the epic delete pre-checks run
--     in the module. The embeddings stay on the board: they need the
--     operator's machine (S1).
--   - X6, the gate. The rules in X1–X4 are covered by the version gate above
--     (sync.allium: config.rules_version).
--
-- Design doc: 2026-10-10-tui-store-split-design.md under the superpowers
-- specs directory (a dated snapshot; this spec is the record).

use "./core.allium" as core
use "./settings.allium" as settings
use "./tasks.allium" as tasks
use "./sync.allium" as sync
use "./feeds.allium" as feeds

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What a dispatch request asks for. Only dispatch exists: wrap-up,
-- exit-session and close have their own table (W1), with their own kind
-- (SessionRequestKind below).
enum RequestKind { dispatch }

-- What a session request asks for (W1): a wrap-up, the close that follows
-- it, or (X1) the teardown of update_tasks' done-path close, which has no
-- wrap-up behind it.
enum SessionRequestKind { wrap_up | exit_session | close }

enum SessionRequestState { requested | running | done | failed }

-- What a feed request asks for: one feed cycle run by the
-- epic's poll owner, or the release of a removed feed task's worktree and
-- window by the host that holds them.
enum FeedRequestKind { refresh | teardown }

enum FeedRequestState { requested | running | done | failed }

-- Which agent a dispatch launches. Fixed on the row when it is written and
-- read from the row by the host, never re-derived there (D3). quick is only
-- ever named by its caller (tasks.allium: CreateQuickTask).
enum DispatchRequestMode { dispatch | research | quick }

enum DispatchRequestState { requested | provisioning | dispatched | failed }

-- Which launch path wrote the request (D9). user is the TUI keys, including
-- retry-fresh and research; mcp is dispatch_task; epic_chain is
-- epics.allium's AutoDispatchNextSubtask; quick is quick dispatch; schedule
-- is a board firing a due task (schedules.allium: FireDueTask).
enum DispatchRequestOrigin { user | mcp | epic_chain | quick | schedule }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- One request to dispatch one task, from the moment a client asks until the
-- host reports or the timer fails it, and kept afterwards as history until it
-- is pruned (RequestRowsArePruned) or its task is deleted
-- (DeletingATaskDeletesItsRequestRows). Stored
-- as the module's dispatch_request table. An absent optional field below is
-- stored as "" rather than as an Option; that is storage, not behaviour.
entity DispatchRequest {
    task: core/Task
    kind: RequestKind
    -- The machine that provisions this request, which is always the machine
    -- that asked for it (D2). Its board's subscription delivers the row
    -- (sync.allium: SubscribeToThisHostsDispatchRequests).
    host: core/Host
    -- The identity of the client whose call wrote the request (D11),
    -- written by the module from the call, never by the client. Always
    -- host.owner when the request was written.
    requested_by: core/UserIdentity
    mode: DispatchRequestMode
    origin: DispatchRequestOrigin
    state: DispatchRequestState
    requested_at: Timestamp
    -- Set by HostClaimsDispatch. Null on a row that failed while still
    -- requested.
    claimed_at: Timestamp?
    finished_at: Timestamp when state = dispatched | failed
    reason: String when state = failed
    worktree: core/Worktree when state = dispatched
    tmux_window: core/TmuxWindow when state = dispatched
    -- Whether provisioning reused a worktree that already existed rather than
    -- creating it, as the host reported it (HostReportsDispatched). Only
    -- provisioning can tell: it measures the directory before acting.
    -- Stored false until then.
    reused_worktree: Boolean

    transitions state {
        requested -> provisioning
        requested -> failed
        provisioning -> dispatched
        provisioning -> failed
        terminal: dispatched, failed
    }

    is_open: state in {requested, provisioning}

    -- ONE deadline, from requested_at, whatever the state (D4). A row that
    -- sat requested for a while before its host claimed it has that much less
    -- time to provision. Not a second timer from claimed_at. Exposed on the
    -- row because the HOST enforces it (dispatch.allium:
    -- ProvisioningStopsAtTheDeadline); the module's own timer
    -- (DispatchRequestTimesOut) is the backstop.
    deadline: requested_at + config.dispatch_request_timeout

    @invariant OnlyTheClaimedRequestProvisions
        -- dispatch.allium's DispatchClaimExclusive, restated for the request
        -- row. A request is written only by the same transaction that claims
        -- its task (RequestDispatch), so a request that is refused wrote
        -- nothing and provisions nothing. Only the board whose claim of the
        -- row was accepted (HostClaimsDispatch) provisions it; a second board
        -- on the same host, or a redelivered row, is refused by the state
        -- check. And a row that has finished cannot be reported on again, so a
        -- worktree provisioned after its request timed out is never recorded
        -- on the task. Not an expression because it constrains the order of
        -- writes, not any single state.
}

-- One wrap_up, exit_session or close request for one task, from the
-- moment the module accepts it until it is done or failed, and kept afterwards
-- as history until it is pruned (RequestRowsArePruned) or its task is deleted
-- (DeletingATaskDeletesItsRequestRows). Stored as the module's session_request
-- table. An absent optional
-- field below is stored as "" rather than as an Option; that is storage, not
-- behaviour.
entity SessionRequest {
    task: core/Task
    kind: SessionRequestKind
    -- The wrap-up action: on a wrap_up row the one asked for, on an
    -- exit_session row the one its token was issued for, on a close row
    -- always done (X1).
    action: core/WrapUpMode
    -- The machine holding the task's worktree and window, whose board does
    -- the host work. Its board's subscription delivers the row (sync.allium:
    -- SubscribeToThisHostsSessionRequests). On a close row: the task's host,
    -- or the closing board's own host when the task has none (X1).
    host: core/Host
    -- The identity of the client whose call wrote the request (W11, D11),
    -- written by the module from the call. Always host.owner when the request
    -- was written.
    requested_by: core/UserIdentity
    state: SessionRequestState
    requested_at: Timestamp
    -- Set by HostClaimsSessionRequest. Null on a row written done (a wrap_up
    -- with action done or pr) and on a row that failed while still requested.
    claimed_at: Timestamp?
    finished_at: Timestamp when state = done | failed
    reason: String when state = failed
    -- On an exit_session row with action pr: the PR url the close recorded on
    -- the task. Null otherwise, a close row included: the host reads a close
    -- row's PR url from the task, as it does for an exit_session row with
    -- action rebase or done (pr-workflow.allium: ExitSessionTeardown).
    pr_url: String?
    -- On an exit_session row: the wrap_up row whose id was the token, which
    -- this row consumed (W3). Null on a wrap_up row and on a close row.
    wrap_up_request: SessionRequest?
    -- On an exit_session or close row: the window the close cleared from the
    -- task, which the host tears down. Null on a wrap_up row, and on a close
    -- row for a task that had no window (written only for its PR url, X1).
    tmux_window: core/TmuxWindow?
    -- On an exit_session row: the subtask the epic chain requested in the
    -- same transaction (epics.allium: AutoDispatchNextSubtask), or null when
    -- the chain stopped. Null on a wrap_up row and on a close row, which
    -- runs no chain.
    chained_task: core/Task?

    transitions state {
        requested -> running
        requested -> failed
        running -> done
        running -> failed
        terminal: done, failed
    }

    is_open: state in {requested, running}

    -- Whether the row needs its host to do anything. A wrap_up with action
    -- done or pr is written already done and never does (W2). A close row is
    -- written only when there is host work (X1).
    has_host_work: kind = exit_session or kind = close or action = rebase

    -- ONE deadline, from requested_at, whatever the state (W7, as D4).
    -- Exposed on the row because the HOST enforces it (pr-workflow.allium:
    -- WrapUpRebase, ExitSessionTeardown); the module's own timer
    -- (SessionRequestTimesOut) is the backstop. Stored "" on a row with no
    -- host work, which is never open and has no timer. A close row is a
    -- teardown and takes the teardown's budget (X1).
    deadline:
        if kind = wrap_up: requested_at + config.wrap_up_rebase_timeout
        else: requested_at + config.exit_session_teardown_timeout

    -- The exit token this row is, while it is one (W3): a done wrap_up row
    -- for its task with no newer done wrap_up row beside it (newer by
    -- requested_at, ties broken by row id), finished after the task's latest
    -- dispatched request (a later dispatch voids it), and with no
    -- exit_session row that consumed it.
    is_valid_token:
        kind = wrap_up and state = done
        and not DispatchRequests.any(d =>
            d.task = task and d.state = dispatched
            and d.finished_at >= finished_at)
        and not SessionRequests.any(r =>
            r.task = task and r.kind = wrap_up and r.state = done
            and r.requested_at > requested_at)
        and not SessionRequests.any(r =>
            r.kind = exit_session and r.wrap_up_request = this)

    @invariant OnlyTheClaimedSessionRequestIsServed
        -- As OnlyTheClaimedRequestProvisions above. Only the board whose
        -- claim of the row was accepted (HostClaimsSessionRequest) runs the
        -- rebase or the teardown; a second board on the same host, or a
        -- redelivered row, is refused by the state check. A row that has
        -- finished cannot be reported on again, so a rebase that finished
        -- after its row timed out issues no token. Not an expression because
        -- it constrains the order of writes, not any single state.
}

-- One request for a host to do feed work, from the moment the
-- module writes it until its host reports it done or failed, and kept
-- afterwards as history until it is pruned (FeedRequestRowsArePruned). Stored as
-- the module's feed_requests table: one table, two kinds. A refresh row asks
-- an epic's poll owner to run one feed cycle (a manual "r" pressed on another
-- host, RequestFeedRefresh); a teardown row asks the host holding a feed
-- task's worktree and window to release them after the stale delete removed
-- the task (StaleDeleteRequestsTeardownOnTheHoldingHost). An absent optional
-- field below is stored as "" (a row reference as 0) rather than as an
-- Option; that is storage, not behaviour.
entity FeedRequest {
    kind: FeedRequestKind
    -- The machine that serves the row. On a refresh row the epic's poll
    -- owner; on a teardown row the removed task's host. Its board's
    -- subscription delivers the row (sync.allium:
    -- SubscribeToThisHostsFeedRequests).
    host: core/Host
    -- The feed epic to run. Stored 0 on a teardown row.
    epic: core/Epic when kind = refresh
    -- The removed task's id, for logs only: the task row is gone by the time
    -- anyone reads this. A bare id, not a reference. Stored 0 on a refresh
    -- row.
    task_id: Integer when kind = teardown
    -- Teardown snapshots, taken from the task row in the same transaction,
    -- BEFORE the delete. A null worktree or window is one the task did not
    -- hold. There is no branch column: the teardown derives the branch from
    -- the worktree, as tasks.allium's TaskTeardown does.
    repo_path: String? when kind = teardown
    worktree: core/Worktree? when kind = teardown
    tmux_window: core/TmuxWindow? when kind = teardown
    -- The identity of the client whose call wrote the request, written by the
    -- module from the call (ctx.sender()), never by the client. A board serves
    -- only rows its own owner requested (feeds.allium:
    -- ServeFeedRefreshRequest, ServeFeedTeardownRequest).
    requested_by: core/UserIdentity
    state: FeedRequestState
    requested_at: Timestamp
    -- Set by HostClaimsFeedRequest, and set again when a running row left by
    -- a board that died mid-serve is claimed again, after
    -- config.feed_request_reclaim_grace.
    claimed_at: Timestamp?
    finished_at: Timestamp when state = done | failed
    reason: String when state = failed

    transitions state {
        requested -> running
        running -> done
        running -> failed
        terminal: done, failed
    }

    is_open: state in {requested, running}

    -- NO DEADLINE AND NO BACKSTOP TIMER, unlike DispatchRequest and
    -- SessionRequest. A teardown row must outlive its host being offline:
    -- the worktree and window it names sit on that machine until its board
    -- next runs, whenever that is, and a timer that failed the row would
    -- strand them for good. A refresh row is bounded instead by the prune
    -- (config.stale_refresh_request_window, FeedRequestRowsArePruned).

    @invariant FeedTeardownRequestsAreForTheHoldingHost
        -- A teardown row names the host that held the removed task's
        -- worktree or window, and is written only when that host is not the
        -- one that ran the ingest, held by a host the caller owns, and only
        -- when the task held something
        -- (StaleDeleteRequestsTeardownOnTheHoldingHost). Not an expression
        -- because the task it describes no longer exists to quantify over.

    @invariant NoDoubleTeardown
        -- No teardown row is written for a task whose host is the ingesting
        -- host: that host tears its own removed tasks down in the cycle
        -- itself, before notifying (feeds.allium: RoleRoutedFeedSync's
        -- teardown fan-out), so a row would tear the same worktree down
        -- twice. Every removed task with a worktree or window is torn down by
        -- exactly one of the two paths. Not an expression for the same reason
        -- as above.

    @invariant OnlyTheClaimedFeedRequestIsServed
        -- As OnlyTheClaimedSessionRequestIsServed, with one difference: a
        -- running row may be claimed again once config.feed_request_reclaim_grace
        -- has passed since its claim (HostClaimsFeedRequest), because both
        -- kinds of work are idempotent and a board that died mid-serve must
        -- not strand its row. Within the grace the claim is refused, so two
        -- boards sharing a host id do not both serve one row. A finished row
        -- cannot be claimed or reported on again.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- How long a dispatch request may stay open, measured from requested_at.
    -- The host stops provisioning at that point and reports failed; the
    -- module fails a row still open then as a backstop
    -- (DispatchRequestTimesOut). A module config value, read by the host
    -- from the row's deadline rather than from a copy of its own. Shorter
    -- than the host's worst-case subprocess budget (several sequential git
    -- calls, each bounded by SUBPROCESS_TIMEOUT) on purpose: the deadline
    -- bounds the dispatch, and a subprocess still running at it is killed.
    -- Dispatch only; session requests have their own (W7).
    dispatch_request_timeout: Duration = 60.seconds

    -- How long after the deadline the module's backstop timer waits before
    -- failing a row still open (DispatchRequestTimesOut). Long enough for a
    -- live host's own stop at the deadline, rollback included, to land
    -- first with "deadline passed"; "timed out" is then only ever the reason
    -- for a host that is gone or hung.
    dispatch_request_backstop_grace: Duration = 10.seconds

    -- How long a wrap_up rebase request may stay open, measured from
    -- requested_at (W7). The host stops the rebase at that point and reports
    -- failed (pr-workflow.allium: WrapUpRebase); the module fails a row still
    -- open then as a backstop (SessionRequestTimesOut). Read by the host from
    -- the row's deadline. A wrap_up with action done or pr has no host work
    -- and no deadline.
    wrap_up_rebase_timeout: Duration = 120.seconds

    -- How long an exit_session request's teardown may stay open, measured
    -- from requested_at (W7). The close itself is already applied by then;
    -- this bounds only the host's window kill (pr-workflow.allium:
    -- ExitSessionTeardown). A close row (X1) is held to the same budget.
    exit_session_teardown_timeout: Duration = 30.seconds

    -- How long after a session request's deadline the module's backstop
    -- timer waits before failing a row still open (SessionRequestTimesOut).
    -- As dispatch_request_backstop_grace: long enough for a live host's own
    -- stop at the deadline to land first.
    session_request_backstop_grace: Duration = 10.seconds

    -- How long a finished request row, of any of the three tables, is kept
    -- for anyone to read before RequestRowsArePruned (dispatch and session
    -- rows) or FeedRequestRowsArePruned (feed rows) may remove it. Measured from the row's
    -- finished_at, or its requested_at when it never recorded one. The
    -- module's REQUEST_RETENTION_DAYS.
    request_retention: Duration = 14.days

    -- How long a refresh FeedRequest may sit requested before
    -- FeedRequestRowsArePruned may remove it. A refresh asked of a
    -- poll owner that has been offline this long is moot: the asker wanted
    -- fresh data then, not whenever the owner returns. The module's
    -- STALE_REFRESH_REQUEST_SECS. A teardown row has no such window.
    stale_refresh_request_window: Duration = 1.hours

    -- How long a FeedRequest must have been running, measured from its
    -- claimed_at, before its host may claim it again (HostClaimsFeedRequest).
    -- A feed command is capped at two minutes and a teardown takes seconds, so
    -- this is past any live serve: a row still running before it is most
    -- likely being served, possibly by a second board on the same host id,
    -- and a row older than it was left by a board that died. The module's
    -- RECLAIM_RUNNING_FEED_REQUEST_SECS.
    feed_request_reclaim_grace: Duration = 10.minutes
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The module's request-row reducers. Any store client may call them; each
-- rule below decides from caller() whether the call stands (D11, W11). The
-- writers are the board's launch paths, the MCP tools and the epic chain
-- (request_dispatch, request_wrap_up, request_exit_session, close_task,
-- request_feed_refresh); the reporters are the host whose board the row
-- names (claim_*, report_*). Rules in other specs that raise one of these
-- emit it qualified (split/DispatchRequested(...)).
surface ModuleRequestReducers {
    facing _: core/StoreClient

    provides:
        DispatchRequested(task, requester, origin, mode?)
        WrapUpRequested(task, host, action)
        ExitSessionRequested(task, token, action, pr_url?)
        CloseTaskRequested(task, host)
        FeedRefreshRequested(epic, host)
        -- The claims: a host's board claiming a row its subscription
        -- delivered (claim_dispatch_request, claim_session_request,
        -- claim_feed_request).
        DispatchRequestDelivered(request)
        SessionRequestDelivered(request)
        FeedRequestDelivered(request, host)
        -- The reports of the claiming host.
        DispatchProvisioned(request, reporter, worktree, tmux_window, reused_worktree)
        DispatchProvisioningFailed(request, reporter, reason)
        SessionRequestDone(request, reporter)
        SessionRequestFailed(request, reporter, reason, conflict)
        FeedRequestDone(request, reporter)
        FeedRequestFailed(request, reporter, reason)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- == Dispatch requests ==

rule RequestDispatch {
    when: DispatchRequested(task, requester, origin, mode?)

    requires: task.status = backlog
    -- The claimability check: the request is provisioned on the
    -- requester's own machine (D2), so a task whose worktree is on another
    -- machine is refused rather than given a second worktree here.
    requires: task.host = null or task.host = requester
    -- Redundant with the backlog check while nothing else moves the task, and
    -- not otherwise: a manual move back to backlog during provisioning leaves
    -- the old request open, and a second one beside it would race it.
    requires: not DispatchRequests.any(r => r.task = task and r.is_open)
    -- D11: only the requester's own owner may name it.
    requires: requester.owner != null and requester.owner = caller()
    -- epics.allium: PhoenixIsNeverChained, enforced where the claim is: the
    -- epic chain's request for a phoenix subtask is refused, with that
    -- reason, like any other refused request. Any other origin may request
    -- a phoenix task.
    requires: not (origin = epic_chain and task.phoenix)
    -- schedules.allium (S8): the epic chain passes over a subtask whose
    -- scheduled time is still ahead. Any other origin may launch it early.
    requires: not (origin = epic_chain and task.next_due != null and task.next_due > now)
    -- schedules.allium: FireDueTask, checked by the store's clock. A
    -- schedule-origin request is for a task that is due now: it guards a board
    -- whose clock runs ahead of the store's, and a board acting on a view
    -- older than a ClearSchedule.
    requires: origin != schedule or (task.next_due != null and task.next_due <= now)

    -- D3: the caller's mode when it names one, else the derivation.
    let chosen_mode =
        if mode != null: mode
        else: if task.has_plan or task.tag != research: dispatch else: research

    -- The claim: one write, conditional on the checks above.
    ensures: task.status = running
    ensures: task.sub_status = default_sub_status(running)
    ensures: current_agent(task).last_pre_tool_use_at = now
    -- The dispatch-claim subagent clear point (agent-health.allium), in the
    -- same transaction as the claim.
    ensures:
        for entry in core/SubagentEntries where task = task:
            not exists entry
    ensures: current_agent(task).live_subagents = 0
    ensures: current_agent(task).stop_pending = false
    -- The epic status rollup for the claimed task's epic chain, in the same
    -- transaction, as every later move of the request does.
    ensures:
        if task.epic != null:
            RecalculateEpicStatus(task.epic)
    -- Retention, before the new row is written.
    ensures: PruneRequestRows(at: now)
    ensures: DispatchRequest.created(
        task: task,
        kind: dispatch,
        host: requester,
        requested_by: caller(),
        mode: chosen_mode,
        origin: origin,
        state: requested,
        requested_at: now
    )

    @guidance
        -- THE CLAIM STARTS AN AGENT (agents.allium: StartAgentOnFreshLaunch,
        -- since A1). In this same transaction the store ends the task's
        -- current agent as replaced, hands out a fresh name from the ledger
        -- and creates the new current agent. A refused claim starts none.
        --
        -- The request reducer. Every launch path that provisions reaches it
        -- (D6): dispatch.allium's DispatchTask and DispatchResearchTask (the
        -- TUI keys and retry-fresh), tasks.allium's CreateQuickTask,
        -- mcp-task-tools.allium's DispatchTaskViaMcp and epics.allium's
        -- AutoDispatchNextSubtask. Resume paths (ResumeTask, RetryResume)
        -- reuse an existing worktree, take no claim and write no request.
        -- requester is the calling client's host, as the store handle
        -- carries it; a client cannot name some other machine as the host.
        --
        -- A refusal is ORDINARY: it is how a caller learns that another entry
        -- point claimed the task first.
        -- The refusal names why (not backlog, owned by another machine, an
        -- open request already exists, a phoenix task or a task still
        -- waiting for its scheduled time the epic chain may not take, a
        -- schedule-origin request for a task that is not due), and nothing
        -- was written. The epic chain treats every refusal
        -- alike and offers its next candidate (epics.allium:
        -- AutoDispatchNextSubtask).
        --
        -- The module also schedules the request's timer here (D4): one row
        -- in a SpacetimeDB schedule table, due at the request's deadline, and
        -- cancelled by whichever rule finishes the request first.
}

rule HostClaimsDispatch {
    when: DispatchRequestDelivered(request)

    requires: request.host = core/local_host()
    requires: request.state = requested
    -- D11: the claim names this host, so only its owner may send it.
    requires: request.host.owner != null and request.host.owner = caller()

    ensures: request.state = provisioning
    ensures: request.claimed_at = now
    ensures: DispatchProvisioningClaimed(request: request)

    @guidance
        -- DispatchRequestDelivered is the subscription delivering a row that
        -- names this host: a live insert, or one of the initial rows when the
        -- board starts (D5). On the initial rows it runs only after
        -- HostRestartFailsInterrupted has failed the rows that were already
        -- provisioning, so a board never fails its own fresh claim.
        --
        -- The board calls the module's claim reducer, which re-checks
        -- state = requested inside the transaction. Of two boards on the same
        -- host, or of a row delivered twice, exactly one claim is accepted,
        -- and only that board provisions (dispatch.allium: ProvisionDispatch).
        -- A refused claim does nothing further.
        --
        -- Only an ANSWERED claim settles the delivery. A claim that could not
        -- be sent, or a row held back because the board's identity has not
        -- settled (ProvisionDispatch's requested_by check needs it), leaves
        -- the row requested and this board's to claim: the next delivery
        -- serves it again, until the row is claimed or the backstop fails it
        -- at its deadline (DispatchRequestTimesOut).
}

rule HostReportsDispatched {
    when: DispatchProvisioned(request, reporter, worktree, tmux_window, reused_worktree)

    requires: reporter = request.host
    requires: request.state = provisioning
    -- D11.
    requires: reporter.owner != null and reporter.owner = caller()
    -- A dispatched report names the worktree it made; an empty one is refused.
    requires: worktree != ""
    -- D4: a dispatched report is too late once the row's deadline has
    -- passed, by the module's clock. The host has stopped by then
    -- (dispatch.allium: ProvisioningStopsAtTheDeadline); a report that raced
    -- it is refused, and the host tears down and reports the failure.
    requires: now < request.deadline

    ensures: request.state = dispatched
    ensures: request.finished_at = now
    ensures: request.worktree = worktree
    ensures: request.tmux_window = tmux_window
    ensures: request.reused_worktree = reused_worktree
    -- The task's provisioning fields, written by the module.
    -- host is written with worktree because core/Task's HostTracksWorktree
    -- invariant pairs the two.
    ensures: request.task.worktree = worktree
    ensures: current_agent(request.task).tmux_window = tmux_window
    ensures: request.task.host = request.host
    -- The epic status rollup for the task's epic chain, in the same reducer.
    ensures:
        if request.task.epic != null:
            RecalculateEpicStatus(request.task.epic)

    @guidance
        -- Also cancels the request's scheduled timer.
        --
        -- A LATE REPORT IS REFUSED. If the timer has already failed the row,
        -- state is failed and this rule does not apply: the task's fields are
        -- not written, and the claim has already been released. The host
        -- then tears down what it made, by the provisioning-failure rollback
        -- (dispatch.allium: ProvisionDispatch), so no agent keeps running
        -- against a task that is back in backlog.
        --
        -- A REPEATED REPORT IS ACCEPTED AND CHANGES NOTHING (see
        -- HostRepeatsDispatchedReport): a host whose report got no answer
        -- sends it again, and must hear "accepted" if the first one landed.
}

rule HostRepeatsDispatchedReport {
    when: DispatchProvisioned(request, reporter, worktree, tmux_window, reused_worktree)

    requires: reporter = request.host
    requires: request.state = dispatched
    requires: request.worktree = worktree
    requires: request.tmux_window = tmux_window
    -- D11.
    requires: reporter.owner != null and reporter.owner = caller()

    @guidance
        -- Idempotence of HostReportsDispatched: the same report on a row it
        -- already dispatched is accepted with no further writes — not the
        -- row, the task, the epic rollup or the timer — whatever the clock
        -- says now. It is what lets the host settle a dispatched report that
        -- ended in a transport error by simply sending it once more
        -- (dispatch.allium: ProvisionDispatch, "A report that ends in a
        -- transport error"). A report naming a different worktree or window,
        -- or on a row that failed, is still refused.
}

rule HostReportsDispatchFailed {
    when: DispatchProvisioningFailed(request, reporter, reason)

    requires: reporter = request.host
    requires: request.state = provisioning
    -- D11.
    requires: reporter.owner != null and reporter.owner = caller()

    ensures: request.state = failed
    ensures: request.finished_at = now
    ensures: request.reason = reason

    @guidance
        -- The host has already rolled back what it created (dispatch.allium:
        -- ProvisionDispatch). Its own deadline stop reports through here too,
        -- with reason "deadline passed" (dispatch.allium:
        -- ProvisioningStopsAtTheDeadline). The claim is released by
        -- ReleaseClaimOfFailedRequest, the same rule every failure uses.
        -- Also cancels the request's scheduled timer.
}

rule DispatchRequestTimesOut {
    when: request: DispatchRequest.deadline + config.dispatch_request_backstop_grace <= now

    requires: request.is_open

    ensures: request.state = failed
    ensures: request.finished_at = now
    ensures: request.reason = "timed out"

    @guidance
        -- The scheduled reducer (D4), firing from the request's own schedule
        -- row. It is the BACKSTOP: a live host stops at the same deadline and
        -- reports "deadline passed" itself (dispatch.allium:
        -- ProvisioningStopsAtTheDeadline), which finishes the row and cancels
        -- this timer. It fires config.dispatch_request_backstop_grace after
        -- the deadline, so a live host's stop and rollback land first. What is
        -- left for this rule is a host that never claimed the row (its board
        -- stopped before it claimed) or one that claimed it and is gone or
        -- hung; "timed out" names only that case. It RELEASES THE CLAIM
        -- (through ReleaseClaimOfFailedRequest): a request past its deadline
        -- is treated as dead, not slow. That is safe because a late
        -- dispatched report is refused
        -- (HostReportsDispatched) and the host tears down, so a second
        -- dispatch of the released task cannot meet a first one that still
        -- records its worktree.
}

rule HostRestartFailsInterrupted {
    when: sync/StoreReachedAtStartup(_)

    -- D11: the sweep names this host, so only its owner may run it.
    requires: core/local_host().owner != null and core/local_host().owner = caller()

    for request in DispatchRequests where host = core/local_host() and state = provisioning:
        ensures: DispatchProvisioningFailed(
            request: request,
            reporter: core/local_host(),
            reason: "host restarted"
        )

    @guidance
        -- D5. A row this host had claimed before the board stopped is not
        -- resumed: the process that was provisioning it is gone, and what it
        -- left on disk is unknown. Reporting it failed releases the claim at
        -- once instead of leaving the task claimed until the timer fires.
        -- Fires on the first connection after the board starts
        -- (sync.allium: FirstConnectionLetsTheBoardDraw), never on a
        -- reconnect, when this board's own provisioning may still be running.
        --
        -- ASSUMPTION: ONE BOARD PER HOST. Startup restarts the board inside
        -- the single dispatch tmux session rather than starting a second one
        -- (startup.allium), so the provisioning rows naming this host when a
        -- board starts belong to a board that is no longer running. A second
        -- live board on the same host would have its in-flight requests failed
        -- by this rule; that configuration is not supported.
}

rule DeletingATaskFailsItsOpenRequest {
    when: tasks/TaskRowDeleted(task)

    let open = DispatchRequests where task = task and is_open

    ensures:
        for request in open:
            request.state = failed
            request.finished_at = now
            request.reason = "task deleted"

    @guidance
        -- D8. In the SAME reducer that deletes the task row, and it cancels
        -- the request's scheduled timer. TaskRowDeleted is raised by every
        -- rule that deletes a task row: DeleteTask and BatchDelete
        -- (tasks.allium), an epic delete's subtree (epics.allium: DeleteEpic,
        -- the super-admin's delete of an unseen epic among them) and the feed
        -- stale delete (feeds.allium: UpsertFeedTasks,
        -- RoleRoutedFeedSync). The module runs all three delete rules from
        -- one list of side effects every such reducer calls
        -- (spacetime/module/src/tasks_epics.rs::delete_task_side_effects).
        -- At most one row matches
        -- (OneOpenDispatchRequestPerTask). ReleaseClaimOfFailedRequest then
        -- finds no task and does nothing.
        --
        -- THE FAILED ROW IS NOT KEPT. The same transaction then deletes the
        -- row (DeletingATaskDeletesItsRequestRows), so no reader ever sees it
        -- failed "task deleted": the host's subscription sees the row
        -- removed. The host learns of it as it always did, from its
        -- dispatched report being refused when its provisioning ends, and
        -- tears down whatever it made (dispatch.allium: ProvisionDispatch, "A
        -- row that fails under this host"). The failure still matters inside
        -- the transaction: it cancels the timer and runs the release.
}

rule ReleaseClaimOfFailedRequest {
    when: request: DispatchRequest.state transitions_to failed

    let task = request.task
    -- A request failed by its task's deletion (DeletingATaskFailsItsOpenRequest)
    -- has no task left to release.
    let still_claimed = exists task and task.status = running and task.worktree = null

    ensures:
        if still_claimed:
            task.status = backlog
            task.sub_status = default_sub_status(backlog)
            -- agents.allium: EndAgentWhenLaunchFails ends the task's current
            -- agent as launch_failed in this same write.
            FailedDispatchReleasedClaim(task: task)
    -- The epic status rollup for the task's epic chain, in the same reducer,
    -- whether or not the release applied. Every failure reaches it: a
    -- reported one, the backstop, a host restart. A deleted task has no chain
    -- left here; its delete rolled the chain up.
    ensures:
        if exists task and task.epic != null:
            RecalculateEpicStatus(task.epic)

    @guidance
        -- The claim release, applied by the module to every failure: a reported one, a timeout, a host restart, a delete, a
        -- close (CloseFailsOpenRequests; the task is already Done by then, so
        -- nothing is released). It is
        -- conditional: a task that was provisioned or moved on
        -- meanwhile is left alone rather than stomped back to backlog. The
        -- rollup runs in the module so no board has to recalculate an epic
        -- after the module moved its subtask (the backstop and the restart
        -- sweep have no board report behind them to do it). The stamp
        -- RequestDispatch wrote stays on the agent the release ends, as
        -- history nothing reads.
}

-- == Session requests: wrap-up and exit-session ==

rule RequestWrapUp {
    when: WrapUpRequested(task, host, action)

    requires: task.status = running or task.status = review
    requires: task.worktree != null
    -- mcp-task-tools.allium's ReviewTasksAreNotWrappedUp, in the module by
    -- decision (W2): a review-tagged task has no wrap-up to perform. The
    -- refusal carries that guarantee's wording (the tag, that a review task
    -- ends at its PR, and the retag way out). pr_review and release_please
    -- are core/TaskTag's values; the store spells them "pr-review" and
    -- "release-please", as every tag column does. The module keeps its own
    -- copy of the review set, so a new review tag is added there too.
    requires: not task.is_review_tagged
    -- The rebase runs where the worktree is, so the row names the task's own
    -- host, and only that host may ask (the MCP endpoint an agent reaches is
    -- its own machine's board).
    requires: task.host = host
    -- D11 (W11): only the host's own owner may name it.
    requires: host.owner != null and host.owner = caller()
    -- W1: at most one open session request per task, of either kind.
    requires: not SessionRequests.any(r => r.task = task and r.is_open)

    -- Retention, before the new row is written.
    ensures: PruneRequestRows(at: now)
    ensures:
        if action = rebase:
            SessionRequest.created(
                task: task,
                kind: wrap_up,
                action: action,
                host: host,
                requested_by: caller(),
                state: requested,
                requested_at: now
            )
        else:
            -- done and pr: no host work, so the row is written finished, in
            -- this call, and is a valid exit token at once (W2, W3).
            SessionRequest.created(
                task: task,
                kind: wrap_up,
                action: action,
                host: host,
                requested_by: caller(),
                state: done,
                requested_at: now,
                finished_at: now
            )

    @guidance
        -- The request_wrap_up reducer, called by the wrap_up MCP tool
        -- (mcp-task-tools.allium: WrapUpViaMcp) only after its host-local
        -- checks have passed (W2), so a refusal there wrote nothing. host is
        -- the calling board's own host, as the store handle carries it.
        --
        -- A refusal names why (not running or review, no worktree, a
        -- review-tagged task, the worktree is on another machine, an open
        -- session request already exists) and writes nothing; the tool returns it as an
        -- INVALID_PARAMS error.
        --
        -- No task field is written here. The Conflict clear waits for the
        -- claim (HostClaimsSessionRequest), just before the rebase, so a request that is never claimed leaves the task as
        -- it was.
        --
        -- For action rebase the module also schedules the request's timer
        -- here (W7): one row in session_request_timeouts, due at the
        -- deadline plus config.session_request_backstop_grace, and cancelled
        -- by whichever rule finishes the request first. A row written done
        -- has no timer.
}

rule HostClaimsSessionRequest {
    when: SessionRequestDelivered(request)

    requires: request.host = core/local_host()
    requires: request.state = requested
    -- D11 (W11): the claim names this host, so only its owner may send it.
    requires: request.host.owner != null and request.host.owner = caller()
    -- The host enforces the deadline (W7), so a row past it is not started:
    -- the claim is refused, and the backstop fails the row.
    requires: now < request.deadline

    let task = request.task
    -- Bound before the ensures, because an `if` inside ensures reads the
    -- resulting state; the clear tests the sub_status the task had when the
    -- claim arrived.
    let was_conflict = request.kind = wrap_up and task.sub_status = conflict

    ensures: request.state = running
    ensures: request.claimed_at = now
    -- The optimistic clear, in the claim: the card stops showing
    -- the conflict while the rebase runs (pr-workflow.allium: WrapUpRebase).
    ensures:
        if was_conflict:
            task.sub_status = default_sub_status(task.status)
            task.updated_at = now
    ensures: SessionRequestClaimed(request: request)

    @guidance
        -- SessionRequestDelivered is the subscription delivering a row that
        -- names this host and is requested: a live insert, or one of the
        -- initial rows when the board starts (W8). On the initial rows it
        -- runs only after HostRestartFailsInterruptedSessionRequests has
        -- failed the rows that were already running. A wrap_up row written
        -- done is never requested and is never delivered as work.
        --
        -- The claim_session_request reducer re-checks state = requested
        -- inside the transaction, so of two boards, or a row delivered twice,
        -- exactly one claim is accepted, and only that board does the host
        -- work (pr-workflow.allium: WrapUpRebase, ExitSessionTeardown). As
        -- for HostClaimsDispatch, only an ANSWERED claim settles the
        -- delivery: an unsent claim leaves the row requested and this
        -- board's to claim again, until the deadline.
}

rule HostReportsSessionRequestDone {
    when: SessionRequestDone(request, reporter)

    requires: reporter = request.host
    requires: request.state = running
    -- D11 (W11).
    requires: reporter.owner != null and reporter.owner = caller()
    -- As D4: a rebase reported done after the row's deadline, by the module's
    -- clock, is too late, and issues no token. A teardown (an exit_session
    -- or close row) is not held to it: the close stands whatever the
    -- teardown does (W5, X1), so a late teardown report only records what
    -- happened.
    requires: request.kind != wrap_up or now < request.deadline

    ensures: request.state = done
    ensures: request.finished_at = now

    @guidance
        -- report_session_request_done. Also cancels the request's timer. A
        -- done wrap_up row is the exit token from this moment (W3); the
        -- waiting wrap_up call hands its id to the agent
        -- (mcp-task-tools.allium: WrapUpViaMcp).
        --
        -- A LATE REBASE REPORT IS REFUSED: on a row the backstop already
        -- failed, or past the deadline. The rebase did happen in git
        -- (pr-workflow.allium: WrapUpRebase); the agent, told the wrap-up
        -- failed, calls wrap_up again, and a second rebase of an
        -- already-rebased branch changes nothing.
        --
        -- A REPEATED REPORT IS ACCEPTED AND CHANGES NOTHING
        -- (HostRepeatsSessionRequestDone), so a host whose report got no
        -- answer sends it again, as for HostRepeatsDispatchedReport.
}

rule HostRepeatsSessionRequestDone {
    when: SessionRequestDone(request, reporter)

    requires: reporter = request.host
    requires: request.state = done
    -- Only a row this host claimed and reported; a wrap_up row written done
    -- had no report to repeat.
    requires: request.claimed_at != null
    -- D11 (W11).
    requires: reporter.owner != null and reporter.owner = caller()

    @guidance
        -- Idempotence of HostReportsSessionRequestDone: accepted with no
        -- further writes, whatever the clock says now. A done report on a
        -- row that failed is still refused.
}

rule HostReportsSessionRequestFailed {
    when: SessionRequestFailed(request, reporter, reason, conflict)

    requires: reporter = request.host
    requires: request.state = running
    -- D11 (W11).
    requires: reporter.owner != null and reporter.owner = caller()
    -- Only a rebase can conflict.
    requires: conflict implies request.kind = wrap_up

    let task = request.task

    ensures: request.state = failed
    ensures: request.finished_at = now
    ensures: request.reason = reason
    -- THE MODULE sets the Conflict sub_status (W2), through
    -- pr-workflow.allium's FinishTaskConflict, in this transaction. Conflict
    -- is legal only under running and review, so a task moved on meanwhile
    -- (or deleted) is left alone.
    ensures:
        if conflict and exists task and (task.status = running or task.status = review):
            RebaseConflict(task: task)

    @guidance
        -- report_session_request_failed. Also cancels the request's timer.
        -- The host has already aborted a conflicted rebase
        -- (pr-workflow.allium: WrapUpRebase); reason is the host's message,
        -- naming the conflicted files on a conflict. Its own deadline stop
        -- reports through here too, with reason "deadline passed".
        --
        -- A failed wrap_up leaves the task where it was, apart from the
        -- Conflict flag: no status move, no token. The agent fixes the cause
        -- and calls wrap_up again, which writes a new row.
        --
        -- A failed exit_session or close row (the teardown failed) does NOT
        -- revert the close (W5, X1): the task stays Done or Review, and the
        -- row records the reason.
}

rule RequestExitSession {
    when: ExitSessionRequested(task, token, action, pr_url?)

    -- token is the wrap_up row the agent was handed (W3).
    requires: token.task = task
    requires: token.is_valid_token
    -- Part of is_valid_token, stated on its own because it is the newest
    -- condition (W3, decided with the user): a dispatch of the task that
    -- finished at or after the token's row voids the token.
    requires: not DispatchRequests.any(d =>
        d.task = task and d.state = dispatched and d.finished_at >= token.finished_at)
    requires: action = token.action
    requires: current_agent(task).tmux_window != null
    requires: action = pr implies (pr_url != null and pr_url != "")
    -- D11 (W11): the row names the task's host, whose board tears the window
    -- down, so only that host's owner may ask.
    requires: task.host != null and task.host.owner != null and task.host.owner = caller()
    -- W1: at most one open session request per task. A wrap_up rebase still
    -- running for this task (a second wrap_up the agent started) holds it.
    requires: not SessionRequests.any(r => r.task = task and r.is_open)

    let window = current_agent(task).tmux_window
    let recorded_pr_url = if action = pr: pr_url else: null

    ensures:
        let close = SessionRequest.created(
            task: task,
            kind: exit_session,
            action: action,
            host: task.host,
            requested_by: caller(),
            state: requested,
            requested_at: now,
            pr_url: recorded_pr_url,
            wrap_up_request: token,
            tmux_window: window
        )
        -- The close itself, in this same transaction: the terminal write,
        -- the epic rollup, the phoenix respawn and the epic chain
        -- (pr-workflow.allium: ExitSession).
        SessionCloseAccepted(task: task, action: action, pr_url: recorded_pr_url, close: close)

    @guidance
        -- The request_exit_session reducer, called by the exit_session MCP
        -- tool (mcp-task-tools.allium: ExitSessionViaMcp). Writing the
        -- exit_session row is what consumes the token: the row names it
        -- (wrap_up_request), and is_valid_token is false from then on. The
        -- module also schedules the row's teardown timer here (W7).
        --
        -- ONE TRANSACTION, SO THE CLOSE IS WHOLE OR ABSENT (W4). A refused
        -- call writes nothing: the task keeps its status and window and the
        -- token stays valid, so the agent may call again once the cause is
        -- fixed. No state exists in which the token is spent but the close
        -- did not land.
        --
        -- Refusals, which the tool returns as INVALID_PARAMS errors carrying
        -- the module's words, in the order the module checks them:
        --   - no unconsumed done wrap_up row for the task (whatever the token
        --     names), or the token's row was already consumed: "no exit
        --     token — call wrap_up first";
        --   - the token names some other row, a done wrap_up row newer
        --     than it exists, or the task was dispatched again after the
        --     token's row finished (a dispatched request of the task whose
        --     finished_at is not earlier, W3): "invalid exit token";
        --   - action differs from the token's: an error naming both actions;
        --   - current_agent(task).tmux_window is null: "task #<id> has no active session",
        --     which the /wrap-up skill treats as success;
        --   - action pr without a pr_url: "pr_url is required for action
        --     'pr'";
        --   - the host check or an open session request: the reason.
        --
        -- The window to tear down is recorded on the row from the task as
        -- this transaction read it, so the read and the clear are atomic.
}

rule SessionRequestTimesOut {
    when: request: SessionRequest.deadline + config.session_request_backstop_grace <= now

    requires: request.is_open
    -- A row with no host work is written done and has no timer.
    requires: request.has_host_work

    ensures: request.state = failed
    ensures: request.finished_at = now
    ensures: request.reason = "timed out"

    @guidance
        -- The scheduled reducer expire_session_request (W7), firing from the
        -- request's own row in session_request_timeouts, refusing any caller
        -- but the module's scheduler. It is the BACKSTOP: a live host stops
        -- at the deadline and reports "deadline passed" itself, which
        -- finishes the row and cancels this timer. What is left is a host
        -- that never claimed the row or is gone or hung.
        --
        -- A timed-out wrap_up leaves the task as it was: a Conflict the claim
        -- cleared is not restored, and none is set. A timed-out exit_session
        -- or close row leaves the close standing (W5, X1); the window may
        -- still be alive (see the open questions).
}

rule HostRestartFailsInterruptedSessionRequests {
    when: sync/StoreReachedAtStartup(_)

    -- D11 (W11): the sweep names this host, so only its owner may run it.
    requires: core/local_host().owner != null and core/local_host().owner = caller()

    for request in SessionRequests where host = core/local_host() and state = running:
        ensures: SessionRequestFailed(
            request: request,
            reporter: core/local_host(),
            reason: "host restarted",
            conflict: false
        )

    @guidance
        -- W8, as D5 and HostRestartFailsInterrupted: the
        -- fail_interrupted_session_requests reducer, on the first connection
        -- after the board starts, never on a reconnect, and on the same
        -- one-board-per-host assumption. A rebase that was running is not
        -- resumed: the process running it is gone, and the worktree may be
        -- mid-rebase. The agent's wrap_up call died with the board, and it
        -- calls wrap_up again. A teardown that was running (an exit_session
        -- or close row) is failed too, and the starting board then kills the
        -- window the row names, best effort (pr-workflow.allium:
        -- KillWindowOfFailedExitSession).
}

rule DeletingATaskFailsItsOpenSessionRequest {
    when: tasks/TaskRowDeleted(task)

    let open = SessionRequests where task = task and is_open

    ensures:
        for request in open:
            request.state = failed
            request.finished_at = now
            request.reason = "task deleted"

    @guidance
        -- W9, as D8: in the SAME reducer that deletes the task row, whichever
        -- route deleted it (TaskRowDeleted), and it cancels the request's
        -- timer. At most one row matches
        -- (OneOpenSessionRequestPerTask). A host still rebasing finds its
        -- report refused.
        --
        -- THE FAILED ROW IS NOT KEPT. The same transaction deletes it
        -- (DeletingATaskDeletesItsRequestRows), so the host's subscription
        -- sees the row removed, never failed. An exit_session or close row
        -- removed this way before its teardown ran therefore does NOT reach
        -- pr-workflow.allium's KillWindowOfFailedExitSession, which fires on a
        -- row seen failing, and the delete's own teardown has no window to
        -- kill either, because the close already cleared it from the task.
        -- The window survives (see the open question below).
}

rule DeletingATaskDeletesItsRequestRows {
    when: tasks/TaskRowDeleted(task)

    ensures:
        for request in DispatchRequests where task = task:
            not exists request
    ensures:
        for request in SessionRequests where task = task:
            not exists request

    @guidance
        -- Every request row of the task, finished or open, of both tables, in
        -- the SAME reducer that deletes the task row, whichever route deleted
        -- it (TaskRowDeleted). It runs
        -- AFTER DeletingATaskFailsItsOpenRequest and
        -- DeletingATaskFailsItsOpenSessionRequest, which cancel the open
        -- rows' timers and run their releases; the deletion then removes
        -- what they failed. Nothing reads a deleted task's requests: the
        -- rows are history about a task that no longer exists, and every
        -- board downloads every row on connect.
        --
        -- NOT FEED TEARDOWN ROWS. A FeedRequest names no task
        -- row, only a task_id for logs, and a teardown row exists to clean up
        -- AFTER its task is gone. The stale delete inside ingest_feed writes
        -- it once this rule has removed the task's own request rows
        -- (StaleDeleteRequestsTeardownOnTheHoldingHost), so it is never swept
        -- with them.
}

rule RequestRowsArePruned {
    when: PruneRequestRows(at)

    -- What check_exit_token still reads, and must outlive the prune (the
    -- proviso the retention question set):
    --   * a task's newest done wrap_up row (by requested_at, ties broken by
    --     row id), when no exit_session consumed it, is a live token;
    let live_tokens = SessionRequests where kind = wrap_up and state = done
        and not SessionRequests.any(r =>
            r.task = task and r.kind = wrap_up and r.state = done
            and r.requested_at > requested_at)
        and not SessionRequests.any(r => r.kind = exit_session and r.wrap_up_request = this)
    -- A finished row's age runs from finished_at, or from requested_at for a
    -- row that never recorded one.
    let cutoff = at - config.request_retention
    let old_sessions = SessionRequests where not is_open
        and (finished_at ?? requested_at) < cutoff
    let old_dispatches = DispatchRequests where not is_open
        and (finished_at ?? requested_at) < cutoff

    --   * a wrap_up row goes when it is finished, old and not a live token;
    let pruned_wrap_ups = old_sessions where kind = wrap_up
        and not (this in live_tokens)

    ensures:
        for request in old_sessions:
            --   * an exit_session row goes only with the wrap_up row it
            --     consumed (alone, it would make that token valid again), or
            --     when that wrap_up row is already gone; a close row goes
            --     on age alone.
            if (request.kind = wrap_up and request in pruned_wrap_ups)
               or (request.kind = exit_session
                   and (request.wrap_up_request in pruned_wrap_ups
                        or not exists request.wrap_up_request))
               or request.kind = close:
                not exists request
    ensures:
        for request in old_dispatches:
            --   * a dispatched row finished at or after a live token's row
            --     is what voids that token, and stays while the token lives.
            if not (request.state = dispatched
                    and live_tokens.any(t =>
                        t.task = request.task and request.finished_at >= t.finished_at)):
                not exists request

    @guidance
        -- Emitted by RequestDispatch (the epic chain's request included) and
        -- RequestWrapUp, before the new row is written: the tables only grow
        -- by a write, so pruning on that write bounds them with no schedule
        -- of their own to keep alive. It sweeps the dispatch_requests and
        -- session_requests tables, for every task and every host, not only
        -- the requesting task's rows. feed_requests has its own sweep
        -- (FeedRequestRowsArePruned), run by the feed writers.
        --
        -- OPEN ROWS ARE NEVER PRUNED, whatever their age; their timers finish
        -- them first.
        --
        -- THE PRUNE NEVER CHANGES A TOKEN'S VALIDITY. Every row
        -- SessionRequest.is_valid_token reads for a live token is kept: the
        -- token row itself, and any dispatched row that voids it. A consumed
        -- token's wrap_up row and the exit_session row that consumed it go
        -- together. "Live" means the task's newest done wrap_up, unconsumed:
        -- an older one is superseded, is no token (is_valid_token, which
        -- check_exit_token reads the same way), and prunes like any row. So
        -- does a dispatched row newer only than a superseded one.
        --
        -- The module's REQUEST_RETENTION_DAYS is config.request_retention.
        -- Implementation: spacetime/module/src/request_retention.rs::prune_terminal_requests.
}

rule FeedRequestRowsArePruned {
    when: PruneFeedRequestRows(at)

    let cutoff = at - config.request_retention

    -- A finished feed request goes on age alone, as a close row does. An
    -- open one is kept, except a refresh row still requested past
    -- config.stale_refresh_request_window. An open teardown row is never
    -- pruned, whatever its age.
    ensures:
        for request in FeedRequests:
            if (not request.is_open
                    and (request.finished_at ?? request.requested_at) < cutoff)
               or (request.kind = refresh and request.state = requested
                   and request.requested_at < at - config.stale_refresh_request_window):
                not exists request

    @guidance
        -- Emitted by the two feed writers, before the new row is written:
        -- RequestFeedRefresh, and the stale delete when it writes a teardown
        -- row (StaleDeleteRequestsTeardownOnTheHoldingHost). It sweeps
        -- feed_requests for every epic and every host. The dispatch and
        -- wrap-up writes do not run it (an open question below).
        --
        -- A FeedRequest has no timer, so the open-row rule bends once: a
        -- refresh its poll owner never picked up is stale after
        -- config.stale_refresh_request_window. A running refresh row is kept
        -- (its board may yet report), and an open teardown row is kept for as
        -- long as its host stays away, because the worktree it names is still
        -- on that machine.
        --
        -- Implementation: spacetime/module/src/feed_request.rs::prune_feed_requests.
}

-- == Feed requests: a refresh asked of the poll owner, a teardown asked of the holding host ==

rule RequestFeedRefresh {
    when: FeedRefreshRequested(epic, host)

    -- host is the asking board's own host, which the caller must act for.
    requires: host.owner != null and host.owner = caller()
    -- An epic the caller cannot see is refused as "does not exist", as for
    -- ClaimPollOwner.
    requires: caller().sees(epic, epic.id)
    requires: epic.feed_command != null
    let owner = core/PollOwner{scope: epic, scope_id: epic.id}
    -- No owner yet: refused ("no poll owner yet"). The asking board then
    -- claims and runs the cycle itself (feeds.allium: ManualFeedTrigger).
    requires: exists owner
    -- The owner runs its own refresh; it never asks itself.
    requires: owner.host != host
    -- Only the same person's machine may be asked: a refresh runs that
    -- person's feed command on that person's host.
    requires: owner.host.owner != null and owner.host.owner = caller()

    let already_requested = FeedRequests.any(r =>
        r.kind = refresh and r.epic = epic and r.host = owner.host
            and r.state = requested)

    ensures:
        if not already_requested:
            PruneFeedRequestRows(at: now)
            FeedRequest.created(
                kind: refresh,
                host: owner.host,
                epic: epic,
                requested_by: caller(),
                state: requested,
                requested_at: now
            )

    @guidance
        -- The request_feed_refresh(epic_id, host) reducer, called by a manual
        -- "r" on a host that is not the epic's poll owner (feeds.allium:
        -- ManualFeedTrigger). A refusal names why and writes nothing.
        --
        -- IDEMPOTENT while the row waits. A refresh row for the epic on the
        -- owner's host is still requested: nothing is written and the call
        -- succeeds, so a second "r" (from this host or another of the same
        -- person's) adds nothing the owner's next cycle would not already
        -- cover (OneRequestedRefreshRequestPerEpic). Once the owner has
        -- claimed that row it read the feed before this ask, so the ask
        -- writes a new row of its own.
        --
        -- No deadline and no timer (FeedRequest). The poll owner serves the
        -- row on its next poll tick (feeds.allium: ServeFeedRefreshRequest);
        -- a row it never picks up is pruned once older than
        -- config.stale_refresh_request_window.
}

rule HostClaimsFeedRequest {
    when: FeedRequestDelivered(request, host)

    requires: request.host = host
    requires: host.owner != null and host.owner = caller()
    -- requested, or running: a running row was left by a board that died
    -- mid-serve, and both kinds of work are idempotent, so it is served
    -- again rather than failed. But only once config.feed_request_reclaim_grace
    -- has passed since its claim: a younger running row is most likely being
    -- served now, perhaps by a second board on the same host id, and serving
    -- it twice would run a teardown twice and end the row failed though it
    -- succeeded.
    requires: request.is_open
    requires: request.state = requested
        or request.claimed_at < now - config.feed_request_reclaim_grace

    ensures: request.state = running
    ensures: request.claimed_at = now
    ensures: FeedRequestClaimed(request: request)

    @guidance
        -- claim_feed_request(id, host). FeedRequestDelivered is the
        -- subscription delivering a row on this host: a live insert, or one
        -- of the initial rows a starting board receives, which is how rows
        -- left open while the board was down are caught up at startup.
        --
        -- THE OPPOSITE OF HostRestartFailsInterruptedSessionRequests. A
        -- session request left running by a dead board is failed, because a
        -- half-done rebase must not be resumed blind. A feed request left
        -- running is claimed again: a feed cycle reconciles against a fresh
        -- emission, and a teardown of a worktree already gone is a no-op, so
        -- repeating either is safe, and failing a teardown row would strand
        -- its worktree for good.
        --
        -- Only an accepted claim lets the board serve the row (feeds.allium:
        -- ServeFeedRefreshRequest, ServeFeedTeardownRequest). A board whose
        -- claim was refused or never reached the store hands the row back, and
        -- offers it again on its next tick, so a transport error does not
        -- drop a request for the life of the board; a running row inside the
        -- grace is offered again every tick until the grace passes.
}

rule HostReportsFeedRequestDone {
    when: FeedRequestDone(request, reporter)

    requires: reporter = request.host
    requires: request.state = running
    requires: reporter.owner != null and reporter.owner = caller()

    ensures: request.state = done
    ensures: request.finished_at = now

    @guidance
        -- report_feed_request_done(id, reporter). No deadline is checked:
        -- FeedRequest has none.
}

rule HostReportsFeedRequestFailed {
    when: FeedRequestFailed(request, reporter, reason)

    requires: reporter = request.host
    requires: request.state = running
    requires: reporter.owner != null and reporter.owner = caller()

    ensures: request.state = failed
    ensures: request.finished_at = now
    ensures: request.reason = reason

    @guidance
        -- report_feed_request_failed(id, reporter, reason). reason is the
        -- host's message: the feed cycle's failure, or the teardown's.
        -- Nothing else in the store changes; a failed refresh leaves the
        -- epic's tasks as the failed cycle left them (feeds.allium:
        -- FeedCommandFailure), and a failed teardown leaves what it could not
        -- remove on disk.
}

rule StaleDeleteRequestsTeardownOnTheHoldingHost {
    when: feeds/FeedTaskRemovedAsStale(task, ingest_host)

    -- The task as it was BEFORE the delete, in the same transaction.
    requires: task.host != null
    requires: task.host != ingest_host
    requires: task.worktree != null or current_agent(task).tmux_window != null
    -- A board serves only rows its own owner requested, so a row for a host
    -- of ANOTHER person would stay open for ever: none is written.
    requires: task.host.owner = caller()

    ensures: PruneFeedRequestRows(at: now)
    ensures:
        FeedRequest.created(
            kind: teardown,
            host: task.host,
            task_id: task.id,
            repo_path: task.repo_path,
            worktree: task.worktree,
            tmux_window: current_agent(task).tmux_window,
            requested_by: caller(),
            state: requested,
            requested_at: now
        )

    @guidance
        -- Inside the stale delete of ingest_feed (feeds.allium:
        -- IngestFeedEmission, UpsertFeedTasks, RoleRoutedFeedSync), once per
        -- removed task that qualifies, in the SAME transaction as the delete
        -- and written AFTER DeletingATaskDeletesItsRequestRows has removed
        -- the task's own request rows, so the new row is not swept with them.
        -- ingest_host is ingest_feed's host argument, which the caller must
        -- act for.
        --
        -- WHO GETS A ROW. A task whose host is the ingesting host gets none:
        -- that board tears it down in the cycle, before notifying
        -- (NoDoubleTeardown). A task with no host was never dispatched and
        -- has nothing to tear down. A task that held neither a worktree nor a
        -- window owes nothing. A task held by a host of another person than
        -- the caller gets none either (KNOWN GAP: a task another person
        -- dispatched under this feed and the setter's ingest then removes
        -- leaves that person's worktree and window behind).
        --
        -- THE WINDOW is the current agent's, read BEFORE the delete: the task
        -- row's own window column is always blank (agents.allium: the session
        -- facts live on the agent), and the delete removes the agent rows.
        -- A task holding a window but no worktree owes a row too.
        --
        -- NOBODY AIMS THE ROW. The snapshot is taken from the task's host,
        -- repo_path and worktree columns, so PatchTaskNeverAimsAnotherHostsTeardown
        -- lets a patch change those only from the owner of the host holding
        -- the task.
        --
        -- Writing the row first sweeps the finished feed_requests rows
        -- (FeedRequestRowsArePruned), as RequestFeedRefresh does.
        --
        -- The row needs no deadline: the holding host's board serves it
        -- whenever it next runs (feeds.allium: ServeFeedTeardownRequest), and
        -- the prune never removes it while open.
}

-- == The done-path close: close_task and update_task's close row ==

rule RequestCloseTask {
    when: CloseTaskRequested(task, host)

    -- No requirement on task.status: reachable from any status, done
    -- included, as MarkTaskDoneViaMcp promises (mcp-task-tools.allium). A
    -- task that no longer exists is refused ("task <id> no longer exists"),
    -- writing nothing. The
    -- request-shape checks (status the only field; a dispatched agent never
    -- closes its own task) are the tool's, made before this call. No host
    -- check guards the close itself: D11 governs only whether the teardown
    -- row is written (X1).

    let was_running = task.status = running
    let was_already_done = task.status = done
    -- Bound before the ensures, because the close clears the window and the
    -- row must name the window as this transaction read it.
    let window = current_agent(task).tmux_window
    let has_pr_url = task.url != null and task.url.url_type = pr
    -- The machine the window is on: the task's own host, or the caller's
    -- when the task has none (a task never dispatched, with a PR linked by
    -- hand).
    let teardown_host = if task.host != null: task.host else: host
    let needs_teardown = window != null or has_pr_url
    -- D11: a row naming a host makes that host's board act, so only its
    -- owner may write one.
    let may_tear_down = teardown_host.owner != null and teardown_host.owner = caller()
    let open = SessionRequests where task = task and is_open

    -- A stuck wrap_up must not block closing (X1). Failed before the close
    -- row is written, so OneOpenSessionRequestPerTask holds throughout.
    ensures:
        for request in open:
            request.state = failed
            request.finished_at = now
            request.reason = "task closed"

    -- The terminal write: ExitSession's fields for action done
    -- (pr-workflow.allium), with the done -> done exception for completed_at.
    ensures:
        task.status = done
        task.sub_status = default_sub_status(done)
        current_agent(task).tmux_window = null
        task.updated_at = now
        if not was_already_done:
            task.completed_at = now
        if was_running:
            current_agent(task).stop_pending = false

    -- The epic status rollup, in the same transaction.
    ensures:
        if task.epic != null:
            RecalculateEpicStatus(task.epic)

    -- schedules.allium: ClosingCancelsThePendingRun.
    ensures: TaskClosed(task: task)

    ensures:
        if needs_teardown and may_tear_down:
            SessionRequest.created(
                task: task,
                kind: close,
                action: done,
                host: teardown_host,
                requested_by: caller(),
                state: requested,
                requested_at: now,
                tmux_window: window
            )

    @guidance
        -- The close_task reducer, called by update_tasks' dedicated
        -- status="done" path (mcp-task-tools.allium: MarkTaskDoneViaMcp)
        -- with this board's own host. Everything above is ONE transaction, so
        -- the close took effect whole or not at all, and the board never
        -- guards a teardown against a close that did not land.
        --
        -- NO TOKEN AND NO CHAIN. No exit token is issued, checked or
        -- consumed, and SessionClosed is not emitted, so the epic chain
        -- (epics.allium: AutoDispatchNextSubtask) does not run: only a close
        -- with a wrap_up behind it chains (EveryExitSessionHadAWrapUp).
        --
        -- THE PHOENIX RESPAWN. A task that ENTERS Done here respawns, in this
        -- transaction (tasks.allium: PhoenixRespawn, on its usual terms). A
        -- done -> done re-close is no transition, so it neither respawns nor
        -- restamps completed_at.
        --
        -- THE CLOSE ROW. Written only when there is host work: a window to
        -- kill or a pr-typed url to close (pr-workflow.allium: ClosePrOnDone).
        -- A re-close of a task already done that carries a pr-typed url
        -- therefore still writes one, and its host's gh pr close meets a PR
        -- already merged or closed, which logs quietly. The module schedules
        -- the row's teardown timer here (W7, config.exit_session_teardown_timeout).
        -- When the teardown host is not the caller's own (a teammate's
        -- machine, a host with no owner, or one not registered at all) there
        -- is no row, and the close is not refused for it: the task closes
        -- and the window, if any, is left to its own machine.
        --
        -- FAILING AN OPEN ROW. A wrap_up rebase still running for the task
        -- is failed "task closed"; its host's later report is refused, as for
        -- any failed row. An exit_session or close row still open (an earlier
        -- close whose teardown has not landed) is failed the same way, and
        -- its host kills the window it names (pr-workflow.allium:
        -- KillWindowOfFailedExitSession). The failure is not D11-checked:
        -- the row may name a teammate's machine, and it is the close, not a
        -- request for host work, that fails it. An open DISPATCH request is
        -- failed too (CloseFailsOpenRequests below).
        --
        -- The tool waits for nothing; its response text is unchanged.
}

rule CloseFailsOpenRequests {
    when: CloseTaskRequested(task, host)

    let open = DispatchRequests where task = task and is_open

    ensures:
        for request in open:
            request.state = failed
            request.finished_at = now
            request.reason = "task closed"

    @guidance
        -- In the same close_task transaction as RequestCloseTask, which fails
        -- the task's open SESSION request the same way; this rule is its
        -- dispatch twin, as DeletingATaskFailsItsOpenRequest is
        -- DeletingATaskFailsItsOpenSessionRequest's. At most one row matches
        -- (OneOpenDispatchRequestPerTask). It cancels the row's timer.
        --
        -- AFTER THE DONE WRITE. The failure runs once the task is Done, so
        -- ReleaseClaimOfFailedRequest finds a task that is no longer a bare
        -- running claim and leaves it Done rather than putting it back in
        -- backlog. Without this rule a task closed while its dispatch was
        -- still provisioning could be reported dispatched, and
        -- HostReportsDispatched would write a worktree, window and host onto
        -- a Done task with an agent running in it. Now that report is refused,
        -- and the host tears down what it made (dispatch.allium:
        -- ProvisionDispatch, "A row that fails under this host").
        --
        -- Not D11-checked, as for the session row: the row may name a
        -- teammate's machine, and it is the close that fails it.
}
rule UpdateTaskDoneWritesACloseRow {
    -- The same update_task call as tasks.allium: UpdateTaskIsOneTransaction.
    when: tasks/TaskUpdateRequested(task, epic?, patch, host)

    -- The store, not the board's cache, decides ClosePrOnDone for an edit
    -- (pr-workflow.allium). update_task takes the caller's host and, when the
    -- call moves the task into Done (status was not done, is done after the
    -- patch) and the task then carries a pr-typed url, writes a close row for
    -- the host to serve. The close is windowless: an edit to Done never killed
    -- a window, and still does not.
    let was_already_done = task.status = done
    let has_pr_url = task.url != null and task.url.url_type = pr
    let teardown_host = if task.host != null: task.host else: host
    let may_tear_down = teardown_host.owner != null and teardown_host.owner = caller()

    let open = SessionRequests where task = task and is_open

    ensures:
        if not was_already_done and patch.status = done and has_pr_url and may_tear_down:
            -- Failed before the close row is written, so
            -- OneOpenSessionRequestPerTask holds throughout.
            for request in open:
                request.state = failed
                request.finished_at = now
                request.reason = "task closed"
            SessionRequest.created(
                task: task,
                kind: close,
                action: done,
                host: teardown_host,
                requested_by: caller(),
                state: requested,
                requested_at: now,
                tmux_window: null
            )

    @guidance
        -- WHY A ROW. A reducer answers only success or failure, so the board
        -- cannot learn from the call whether it performed the Done transition.
        -- Its subscription cache cannot tell either: it is stale when another
        -- host already moved the task (a repeat close) or the row has just
        -- arrived (a missed one). The module reads its own prior row, so
        -- both are decided where the transition happens.
        --
        -- THE FINAL URL. The url is the task's after the patch, so a call
        -- that attaches a pr-typed url and moves to Done together is caught.
        --
        -- SERVED LIKE ANY CLOSE ROW. The host runs gh pr close best effort
        -- (pr-workflow.allium: ExitSessionTeardown), reading the task when it
        -- serves the row and closing only if it is still done. D11 applies as
        -- in RequestCloseTask: a teammate's host, a host with no owner or an
        -- unregistered one gets no row, and the task still goes Done. An open
        -- session request on the task is failed "task closed" only when the
        -- row is written, unlike RequestCloseTask, which fails it whatever.
        -- patch_task writes no row: it
        -- has no host argument, and no board route uses it for a status
        -- edit.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Host-scoped setting writes
------------------------------------------------------------

-- D11 holds for every reducer that takes a caller-supplied host: the host it
-- names must be registered with an owner equal to caller(). The setting writes
-- are checked here; the other host-checking reducers are listed under D11.

rule SaveSettingForHost {
    when: settings/SettingSaved(host, key, value)

    -- D11: a host's settings are written only by its owner. A setting
    -- can name a script path the host's board runs (the feed commands).
    requires: host.owner != null and host.owner = caller()

    ensures: core/Setting.created_or_updated(host: host, key: key, value: value)

    @guidance
        -- save_setting. An upsert on (host, key) (settings.allium:
        -- SaveSetting). The only rule that creates or changes a setting row:
        -- SetManagedFeedSettingsForHost writes through it.
}

rule ClearSettingForHost {
    when: settings/SettingCleared(host, key)

    -- D11: as SaveSettingForHost. An unowned or unregistered host is
    -- refused even when the key was never set.
    requires: host.owner != null and host.owner = caller()

    ensures: not exists core/Setting{host: host, key: key}

    @guidance
        -- clear_setting. A key that was never set is a no-op for an owner,
        -- not a refusal (settings.allium: ClearSetting).
}

rule SetManagedFeedSettingsForHost {
    when: settings/ManagedFeedSettingsWritten(host, writes)

    -- D11: as SaveSettingForHost.
    requires: host.owner != null and host.owner = caller()
    -- Every write is judged before any lands: a managed-feed key, named
    -- once, and an interval that is a whole number.
    requires: writes.all(w => w.key in settings/config.managed_feed_setting_keys)
    requires: writes.all(w => (writes where key = w.key).count = 1)
    requires: writes.all(w => w.value = null
        or w.key not in settings/config.managed_feed_interval_keys
        or is_whole_number(w.value))

    ensures:
        for write in writes:
            if write.value != null:
                settings/SettingSaved(host: host, key: write.key, value: write.value)
            else:
                settings/SettingCleared(host: host, key: write.key)

    @guidance
        -- set_managed_feed_settings; twin: apply_set_managed_feed_settings.
        -- One reducer call, so every write lands or none does: a command and
        -- its interval are never left out of step (settings.allium:
        -- SetManagedFeedSettings).
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- D1: a task holds at most one open request. RequestDispatch refuses a second
-- one while the first is open.
invariant OneOpenDispatchRequestPerTask {
    for a in DispatchRequests:
        for b in DispatchRequests:
            a != b and a.task = b.task implies not (a.is_open and b.is_open)
}

-- W1: a task holds at most one open session request, of either kind.
-- RequestWrapUp and RequestExitSession refuse a second one while the first is
-- open.
invariant OneOpenSessionRequestPerTask {
    for a in SessionRequests:
        for b in SessionRequests:
            a != b and a.task = b.task implies not (a.is_open and b.is_open)
}

-- W3: an exit token is consumed once. No two exit_session rows name the same
-- wrap_up row.
invariant AnExitTokenIsConsumedOnce {
    for a in SessionRequests:
        for b in SessionRequests:
            a != b and a.kind = exit_session and b.kind = exit_session
                implies a.wrap_up_request != b.wrap_up_request
}

-- W4: every exit_session row consumed a done wrap_up row of its own task, so
-- every close through exit_session had a wrap_up behind it. It applies to
-- exit_session rows ONLY (X1): a close row is a close with no wrap_up behind
-- it, by design, and CloseRowsCarryNoTokenAndNoChain below states what it
-- carries instead.
invariant EveryExitSessionHadAWrapUp {
    for r in SessionRequests:
        r.kind = exit_session implies (
            r.wrap_up_request != null
            and r.wrap_up_request.kind = wrap_up
            and r.wrap_up_request.state = done
            and r.wrap_up_request.task = r.task)
}

-- An epic holds at most one REQUESTED
-- refresh request per serving host. RequestFeedRefresh writes nothing while one
-- waits to be claimed, because the owner reads the feed after that ask. A
-- running row does not count: its owner read the feed before this ask, so the
-- ask needs a cycle of its own. Scoped to the host because the coalescing check
-- reads the current poll owner's rows: a row left requested on a host the poll
-- has since moved away from does not block a new one for the new owner (see
-- the open question below).
invariant OneRequestedRefreshRequestPerEpic {
    for a in FeedRequests:
        for b in FeedRequests:
            a != b and a.kind = refresh and b.kind = refresh
                and a.epic = b.epic and a.host = b.host
                implies not (a.state = requested and b.state = requested)
}

-- X1: a close row consumed no token and ran no chain, and its action is
-- always done.
invariant CloseRowsCarryNoTokenAndNoChain {
    for r in SessionRequests:
        r.kind = close implies (
            r.action = done
            and r.wrap_up_request = null
            and r.chained_task = null
            and r.pr_url = null)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "Teardown window left alive by a task delete: DeletingATaskDeletesItsRequestRows removes an exit_session or close row in the same transaction that fails it, so a host whose teardown had not yet run sees the row vanish rather than fail, KillWindowOfFailedExitSession never fires, and the delete's own teardown has no window to kill (the close already cleared it from the task). Should a host also kill the window of a teardown row it saw open and then sees removed, should the delete keep an open teardown row until its host has acted on it, or is a surviving window acceptable in this narrow case (a task deleted within the teardown's budget of its close)?"

open question "A close failing a chained dispatch: CloseFailsOpenRequests fails an open epic_chain dispatch request 'task closed', and epics.allium's ReportChainedDispatchFailure raises an auto-dispatch failure for every failed epic_chain row whose task still exists, so closing a subtask while its chained dispatch provisions shows the operator a chain failure for a close someone chose. Should ReportChainedDispatchFailure skip the 'task closed' reason, as it skips a deleted task?"

open question "Window left alive (W5, W7, W8): an exit_session or close row the backstop fails because its board was closed or hung, or one the restart sweep fails, may leave the agent's tmux window alive on a task that is already Done or Review. The starting board kills the window of a row its own sweep failed (a row failed by deletion is removed with it, a separate open question above). Should a starting board also kill the windows of teardown rows that failed 'timed out' while it was not running (W8 otherwise treats them as history, as D12 does), or is a surviving window acceptable?"

open question "Seeing other machines' requests: a board's subscription delivers only request rows naming its own host. A teammate's board that shows the same task in a shared epic cannot see the request, so its UnprovisionedIndicator falls back to the claim stamp. Should boards also receive the open requests of every task they can see?"

open question "Feed request prune emitters: FeedRequestRowsArePruned runs only from request_feed_refresh and from the stale delete that writes a teardown row, not from request_dispatch or request_wrap_up. A person who dispatches and wraps up but whose feed epics only run on one host therefore prunes finished teardown rows only when a stale delete writes the next one. Should every request write sweep all three tables, or is the sweep at the two feed writers acceptable?"

open question "update_tasks' done path when close_task gets NO ANSWER: the board treats a no-answer like a refusal and the tool replies that the task could NOT be moved to done and is still in its previous status. The close may in fact have landed. A retry is a done -> done re-close (no restamp, no respawn), but for a task with a pr-typed url it writes a second close row and runs gh pr close again. Should the tool settle a no-answer by re-reading the task, as exit_session settles its own no-answer by looking for the row (mcp-task-tools.allium: ExitSessionViaMcp), or is the 'try again' reply acceptable?"
